  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening responsiveness of health services provision to extreme weather events for urban marginalised people (Urban SHADE).</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Urban SHADE project &#x2013; otherwise known as Strengthening Responsiveness of Health Service Provision to Extreme Weather Events for Urban Marginalised People is a three-year project that aims to improve health service delivery during and after extreme weather events in selected informal settlements in India, Kenya, and Sierra Leone. The project is funded by the United Kingdom&apos;s National Institute of Health and Care Research (NIHR) as part of the Research and Innovation for Global Health Transformation funding stream. NIHR is a major funder of high-quality global health research that directly addresses diverse health priorities across LMIC settings. Urban SHADE partners in Sierra Leone, including the Sierra Leone Urban Research Centre, the Institute of Gender and Children&#x2019;s Health Research, and the Centre of Dialogue for Human Settlement and Poverty Alleviation will work with  partner institutions in India, Kenya, with overall project leadership from  the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine. The project will also work with diverse stakeholders in Sierra Leone to collaboratively design interventions to enhance healthcare access during climate related weather events.</narrative>
  </description>
